<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<RecordType xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description> (255-char cap), and it is
        INSIDE the root element on purpose - a comment ABOVE <RecordType> breaks `sf`
        deploy at conversion.

        THIS FILE REPLACES Opportunity/recordTypes/Commercial.recordType-meta.xml.
        It is a byte-for-byte clone of that file with three substitutions - the root
        <fullName>, <businessProcess> and <label> - plus this comment and the
        <description>. Nothing else was changed, deliberately: every picklistValues block
        (Asset_Type__c, Deal_Category__c, Deal_Status__c, ForecastCategoryName, LeadSource,
        Rejection_Reason__c, Type, Underwriting_Status__c) is identical, so the rename
        cannot quietly alter which values a commercial deal may take. Diff the two files
        before D4; the only expected difference beyond the three substitutions is the
        transitional Deal_Type__c value noted below.

        DEVELOPERNAME CHANGES, NOT ONLY THE LABEL. Apex resolves record types by
        DeveloperName (RecordTypeSelector, LeadConvertService), so a label-only change
        would leave every resolution site saying 'Commercial' forever. The spec approved a
        FULL rename; D3 is where that is paid for.

        D4 IS DONE. The transitional Deal_Type__c 'Commercial' enumeration that this
        block used to carry through the D1->D4 window has been REMOVED, together with the
        Commercial record type and business process. Do not re-add it: after the migration
        no row holds the value and the value no longer exists on the field. If a record
        still renders a blank Deal Type, the cause is a missed migration row, not a
        missing enumeration - re-run the [MIG] verification queries in the runbook.
        (Historical note, kept because it explains why the value was ever here: Apex DML
        does NOT enforce record-type picklist restriction (ARCHITECTURE.md sec 2), so
        omitting it during the window would not have blocked anything - it would only have
        made the UI disagree with the stored value.)
    -->
